{
  "abstract": "We estimate fourth traces of Gaussian quartic character matrices with ordinary physical rows. For squarefree columns of length $B\\le Y$, the trace is $O((YB)^\\epsilon(Y^4B+Y^{-7/2}B^9))$. A coupled radical and sharp-part decomposition, complete coprimality projectors and prime dilation give $\\operatorname{Tr}K_L^4\\ll L^{26/5+\\epsilon}$ at equal row and column lengths. Quadratic modulation of a pointwise bounded coefficient vector then has joint mean $L^{67/40+\\epsilon}$, saving $3/40$ from the generic exponent $7/4$. We restore all ordinary columns by an exact imprimitive Gauss identity, including principal fourth powers and shared supports. Applying the result through the original Poisson transform yields a mixed polynomial moment for two ordinary common coefficient sequences $N^{147/20+\\epsilon}$ at row length $N^4$ and factor lengths $N,N^2$, saving $3/20$ from $N^{15/2}$. A second-trace comparison and finitely many short moments complete an interval cover in a buffered zero detector. This gives natural-family zero-density exponent $11/15$ at real part $3/4$, with sufficiently small fixed height power, compared with $3/4$ from the ordinary fourth moment. The coefficient restriction is essential; no improved arbitrary-coefficient joint operator norm is asserted. Complete finite Gauss controls and rational exponent checks accompany the proof. These results concern family moments and zero counts, and give no individual zero-free half-plane or resolution of the Riemann hypothesis.",
